[shift of finite type]
A \textit{shift of finite type} with alphabet $\mathcal{A}$ is any shift space of $\mathcal{A}^{\mathbb{Z}}$ defined by excluding a finite number of finite words. In other words, $X \subseteq \mathcal{A}^{\mathbb{Z}}$ is a shift of finite type if for some $n \geq 1$ there exists a finite set $\mathcal{F} \subseteq \mathcal{A}^n$ of forbidden words such that
\begin{equation*}
    X = \{x \in \mathcal{A}^{\mathbb{Z}} \,:\, \sigma^m(x)_{[0,n-1]} \notin \mathcal{F}, \mbox{ for all } m \in \mathbb{Z}\}
\end{equation*}

One could choose $\Gamma$ consisting of holonomies of the form $\psi_{u,v, a, b}$, where $u,v \in \mathcal{B}_{b-a + 1}(X)$, for some $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}$ with $a \leq b$, and
\[
\psi_{u,v, a, b}(x) = 
\begin{cases}
x_{(-\infty, a)} u x_{(b, \infty)}, & \text{ if } x_{[a,b]} = v \text{ and }  x_{(-\infty, a)} u x_{(b, \infty)} \in X \\

x_{(-\infty, a)} v x_{(b, \infty)}, & \text{ if } x_{[a,b]} = u \text{ and }  x_{(-\infty, a)} v x_{(b, \infty)} \in X \\

x, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\] 
That is, $\psi_{u,v, a, b}$ replaces $u$ with $v$, or vice versa, whenever possible, and otherwise does nothing.
